A||||5||Adrift||Speaker placement question:||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||09-24-2001||12:40 PM||||Is it better to put a pair of 6x9's in the doors and tweeters in the dash, or the exact opposite? In my pathfinder the tweeters are in the dash and the 6x9s are in the door, and in my friends Acura the tweeter are on the floor by your feet and there are 6x9s in the door.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SLYGUY||09-24-2001||02:00 PM||||I just added a Kenwood deck and changer. I had to replace the rear 4x6s behind the driver and passenger's headrest. They were both blow. <P>Turns out that one of the 4x10s in the dash is borderline blown also. <BR>I want to put 6x9s in the door and tweets in the corners where the rearview mirrors are but I don't want to carve this thing up. I'm resisting that temptation for now. <P>Biggest concern is NO bass. There are limited places to mount any 10-12" subs for a little bass.<BR>Let me know what you end up doing.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||09-24-2001||02:14 PM||||It is better to have the tweeters high and the bass low. Reason is bass frequencies are "omni-directional" Which means that regardless of which direction the speaker points the sound is heard. Tweeters are directional. You can bounce them off of glass and angle them up or down. <BR>To set an example, When the "Thumpers" drive down the street with window closed you can hear the bass but not the higher frequencies. Even with the windows down you cannot hear the highs until the car gets close enough and your angle to the speaker decreases. Just turn up ypur radio fade to the front speakers and walk around your car. You will hear the bass all around but the highs will fade up and down depending on your position relative to the speakers and glass.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieronv||09-24-2001||07:52 PM||||The first comment I would like to make is I think that you will have a hard time fitting 6x9 speakers in the door. Orief is correct in his post about how lows and highs work. To get the best balance between left and right you need a stereo spectrum analyzer and patience (it took me 2 months to aim my mid range and tweeters).If you want mid bass speakers in the door I would recommend 6 or 6.5 mid bass driver (alpine sony kenwood etc)you will be much happier with this than a 6x9 in look as well as performance. If you want to mount the 6x9's in the dash for a better replacement of the 4x10's. This will get better mid bass than the 4x10's. <BR> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2_WEB_1.jpg">  <BR>85GT (twoloud)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||09-24-2001||09:29 PM||||my tweeters are a little off the floor in pods with my mids (5 1/4") where the dead pedal is. i was going for the equal pathlenth deal. it's unscientific, but for me i place them and sit in a normal driving/listening position. if i can see them (like look right at them with out the lower dash or something blocking them), i can hear them. sounds good.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gargoyle||09-25-2001||03:53 AM||||Mount the tweeter high as per the reasons stated by Oreif. If you are going into the doors, go with rounds. You will get far better sound from even a cheap pair of rounds versus ovals. Check into the speaker pods that Aus sells, these would be a great way to go. A 4x6 plate will fit in the stock dash position with a 4" & 1" dome tweeter. Then you could go with a 6" for bass in the door and subs behind the seats. There is plenty of room, you just have to be creative. If you want to go with a sub in the middle I believe Monkeyman is selling his Aus sub center counsole.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
